	doi = {10.1016/j.ijhydene.2025.152383},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020913033?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Due to the high operating frequency and long driving mileage of buses, fuel cell buses demand higher energy utilization efficiency and greater stability in their power systems. To enhance fuel-cell bus operating efficiency and energy economy, the study focuses on jointly optimizing the energy-management strategy and key powertrain parameters. First, determine the power system topology and basic parameters of the fuel cell bus. Utilizing data collected from road tests, models are developed to analyze the drive motor's efficiency and the correlation between fuel cell output power and its efficiency, and the charge-discharge characteristics of lithium batteries. Subsequently, an energy management strategy based on Model Predictive Control (MPC) is formulated, integrating the prediction of vehicle speed. The Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) algorithm is employed to optimize key parameters of the Radial Basis Function (RBF) neural network, resulting in a PSO-enhanced RBF neural network (PSO-RBF) vehicle velocity prediction model. Aiming to reduce energy consumption to the minimum, a novel energy management strategy based on MPC is introduced, which incorporates PSO-RBF speed prediction (PSO-RBF-MPC). The best sequence of control actions is derived through a dynamic programming method and confirmed via simulations using the developed vehicle model. Finally, a power system parameter optimization model is established for fuel cell buses, with the optimization objectives being equivalent hydrogen consumption and time taken to accelerate from 0 to 50 km per hour. Simulation results after optimization indicate that the 0–50 km/h acceleration time is reduced by 6.75s, and there is a reduction of 0.716 kg in the equivalent hydrogen consumption for every 100 km traveled. © 2025 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC},
