	doi = {10.1109/TCPMT.2026.3684918},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036335476?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {To address the potential for mechanical failure of conventional solder interconnects under vibration, this study proposes a PDMS-based sealing architecture for gallium-indium liquid metal (LM) interconnects, ensuring both containment and electrical continuity under mechanical loading. The research focuses on the structural design, assembly feasibility, and mechanical response of the encapsulation. The structure employs a PDMS elastic encapsulation integrated with a symmetric one-way valve for compliant LM containment, forming an electrical pathway through pressure contact between copper pin headers and PCB pads. A fluid-structure interaction (FSI) finite element model was established using Ansys Workbench to analyze a board-level PCB system with 15 interconnected chips under random vibration loading, following the MIL-STD-810H standard (5–2000 Hz). The mechanical response of the structure was evaluated under random vibration excitation. Orthogonal test design (L16(4<sup>3</sup>)) and response surface optimization were employed to refine key structural parameters (outer diameter, height, array pitch), minimizing peak von Mises stress. Results indicate that the maximum stress is 31.847 MPa, demonstrating the structural integrity of the PDMS encapsulation under standard vibration profiles. The LM exhibits uniform wall pressure distribution, with a magnitude of only 2.03 × 10<sup>5</sup> Pa. The study confirms that the gallium-indium alloy interconnect structure achieves mechanical robustness and containment efficiency by “decoupling mechanical bonding from electrical interconnection.” This provides a novel vibration-resistant solution specifically tailored for connector and board-level compliant interfaces, with promising potential for board-adjacent 3D packaging applications. © 2011-2012 IEEE.},
