	abstract = {Maintaining transient stability is essential to ensuring the secure operation of power systems. With the increasing penetration of renewable energy sources, the inherent randomness and uncertainty in system operation have significantly intensified, posing new challenges to stability control. To address this issue, this paper proposes a transient stability preventive control method based on a Transformer model and the Integrated Gradients algorithm. Specifically, a Transformer-based transient stability assessment model is first developed to enable fast classification of system stability status. Then, by integrating this surrogate model with the Integrated Gradients algorithm, the sensitivity of transient stability with respect to the active power outputs of individual generators is approximately estimated. Based on the ranking of generator sensitivities, a hierarchical power adjustment control strategy that incorporates similar stable historical samples is proposed to guide generator dispatch. Finally, case studies on the IEEE 39-bus system, a modified variant integrated with Inverter-Based Resources, and a real-world 496-bus system in China demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed approach. The results demonstrate that the proposed data-driven algorithm achieves high computational efficiency and practical applicability. © 1972-2012 IEEE.},
