	abstract = {This study proposes an adaptive control framework for electric power steering (EPS) systems to address robustness issues and distorted road condition feedback in conventional systems during stationary and near-stationary steering maneuvers. By integrating super-twisting algorithm with bidirectional long short-term memory (Bi-LSTM) network, the framework achieves accurate road condition identification and adaptive haptic feedback using standard EPS sensor configurations. A cascaded structure of high-order super-twisting observers (C-HOSTO) reconstructs critical states, and the Bi-LSTM network identifies road friction. An adaptive steering feel design and a radial basis function neural network-based super-twisting integral sliding mode control (RBF-ST-ISMC) strategy enable road-adaptive torque feedback generation and robust tracking. The overall system stability is guaranteed through rigorous Lyapunov analysis. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed C-HOSTO and Bi-LSTM framework achieves a road identification accuracy exceeding 98 %, representing an improvement of more than 34 percentage points over a conventional backpropagation (BP) network. The proposed RBF-ST-ISMC strategy reduces the root mean square error (RMSE) of desired steering torque tracking by over 16 % and 37 % compared to conventional integral sliding mode control (ISMC) and super-twisting sliding mode control (ST-SMC), respectively. It also reduces the control input total variation (TV) by over 30 % compared to ISMC, while maintaining control input smoothness comparable to ST-SMC. Vehicle experiments on high- and low-friction roads validate the framework's practical effectiveness, achieving steering torque tracking RMSEs below 0.054 Nm and maximum absolute errors below 0.172 Nm. The results demonstrate accurate desired torque tracking and effective road-adaptive steering feel. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
