	abstract = {The transition to a low-carbon energy system necessitates integrating Variable Renewable Energy Sources (VRES) with efficient storage solutions to address their inherent variability and non-dispatchability. This study investigates the economic and environmental performance of Multi-Energy Systems (MES) incorporating both short-term and long-term energy storage technologies under uncertainty. A multi-objective optimization framework explores trade-offs between Levelized Cost of Energy (LCOE) and Carbon Intensity (CI) across various emission targets, while uncertainty analysis is performed using Polynomial Chaos Expansion. Results show that the hybrid configuration of hydrogen storage, batteries, and Thermal Energy Storage achieves the lowest LCOE at zero emissions (0.446 €/kWh), which is critical for balancing renewable energy fluctuations while achieving cost-effective zero-emission goals. Hydrogen storage prevents oversizing of VRES and batteries, enhancing economic feasibility, while short-term storage solutions improve system reliability and efficiency. Sobol’ indices reveal that LCOE is highly sensitive to electricity prices (up to 85%) in cost-optimal solutions, while photovoltaic system (up to 85%) and hydrogen storage costs (up to 48%) dominate in emission-optimal solutions. Multi-criteria analysis on LCOE, CI, and Coefficient of Variation identifies the optimal configuration as a hydrogen-based system targeting 15 gCO<sub>2</sub>/kWh emissions. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
